  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 AM EDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nNear saturated or already saturated surface conditions with clear \noverhead skies and light winds should promote another round of \nmorning fog today before the Sun can help dissipate the shallow \ninversion as the day progresses. Once fog departs from our valleys, \ntemperatures will rocket back upwards, reaching the first of the \nthree hot days of the week. This trend continues through Thursday as \nthe jet stream moves off to the north and a summer strength upper \nridge sets up and dominates over the southern US. Temperatures will \nbe flirting with daily record highs during the middle of the week.\n\nDate    Chattanooga    Knoxville      Tri-Cities     Oak Ridge           \n10-13   88(1954)       89(1935)       86(1954)       84(2017)       \n10-14   87(1954)       87(1881)       85(1954)       85(1954)       \n10-15   85(1950)       87(1897)       83(1971)       83(2021)     \n\nThere is some low potential for a rain shower Thursday afternoon, \nbut should be confined to higher terrain with guidance depicting \nvery meager CAPE profiles.\n\nA phased upper trough will bring about a polar front into the \nEastern US for Friday. This cold front won't bring about much, if \nany, precipitation, but will usher in a breezy and significant \nairmass change for the whole region. For the Tri-Cities, this is \nright on time to experience the first bout of temperatures favorable \nfor frost formation. Those with temperature sensitive plants should \nconsider bringing them in definitely for Saturday night, but also \nconsider Friday and Sunday nights with also chilly lows, especially \nin northern locales. Temperatures then will slightly rebound going \ninto early next week as we maintain a below average precipitation \nstance for the foreseeable future.",
